gs-native-pc (patch 68): the vertex ring waits instead of overwriting, and depth re-attachment is counted

Two things found while auditing the ring added by patch 59.

1. Fence timeout. A wrap waits 1 s on the previous lap's fence; if the wait timed out the code went on and
   reset the offset anyway, so a GPU still reading that lap would get vertices overwritten under it - torn
   geometry that looks like a driver bug. It now blocks for real (GL_TIMEOUT_IGNORED) and counts the stall
   (`ringStalls` in GOW_GS_HW_STATS). A stall is a bug in the ring sizing, never something to ignore.

2. Depth vs ZBP. A RenderTarget owns exactly one depth texture, but it is attached for whatever ZBP the draw
   uses; when ZBP changes the texture keeps the previous buffer's contents (GS keeps one region per ZBP, so
   that is not the same memory). Adding the counter first, before changing the behaviour:
   0.5x Asia disc, 30 s -> `zbpchg=2032` per 5 s, i.e. ~17 re-attachments per frame, so the game does switch
   ZBP on a live target. Counted, not yet fixed: a fix has to key depth by ZBP and keep the Z-sampling path
   (findReinterpretSource, the inverted-Z shadow passes) pointing at the right one, which needs its own patch
   and a frame comparison. `depthCleared` is declared in RenderTarget and never read - it was presumably meant
   for this.

Verified: builds clean; 30 s Asia-disc run at 0.5x with PS2X_GS_WORKER=1 reports ringWraps=12, ringStalls=0,
oversized=0, zbpchg=2032 and the same frame distribution as before (median 28.2 guest fps), so nothing here
changes the normal path.

